# Security Considerations {#security-considerations}

## Trust Model

The MTA acts only as an HTTP client and exposes no endpoint to scanners. This removes attacks such as registration floods against the MTA and request forgery through callbacks. The scanner operator decides which MTAs may register and issues credentials; the MTA operator decides which scanners to call and what each may change.

## Authority of Scanners {#authority}

A scanner can only change what its grants cover, and by default only toward stricter processing. Grants that relax ordered properties, change routes, or rewrite content make the scanner part of the trusted base for those decisions: a compromised scanner with full routing authority can redirect or exfiltrate mail, and one with "relax" on "/action" can let malicious mail through. Operators SHOULD give such grants only to scanners they trust accordingly, limit them with subscription filters, and log their use. The MTA MUST enforce grants itself and MUST NOT rely on scanners respecting them.

Scanner-chosen next hops and ports can also direct the MTA's connections at internal services. An MTA SHOULD restrict them by configuration, for example through the "values" of the grant on "nextHop", and MUST apply to them the same address restrictions it applies to next hops found in the DNS.

## Credentials

Credentials SHOULD be issued per MTA instance, rotated, revoked when an instance is decommissioned, and kept out of logs and diagnostics. Bearer tokens MUST have at least 128 bits of entropy, and the security considerations of {{RFC6750}} apply. Scanners SHOULD apply authorization per MTA identity at registration and SHOULD rate limit registrations per identity. The discovery document is unauthenticated and SHOULD be rate limited.

## Denial of Service

Slow or failing scanners can tie up MTA resources. MTAs MUST enforce timeouts and SHOULD limit concurrent requests per scanner. Delayed replies hold SMTP connections open; besides capping delayMs ({{smtp-response}}), the MTA SHOULD limit how many delayed replies it serves at once. Recipients added at the "data" stage can amplify a message, so MTAs SHOULD limit how many a patch may add. Scanners SHOULD limit request rate, concurrency, and body size per registration.

## Injection {#injection}

The MTA MUST validate everything a patch writes: header field names and values MUST NOT allow injection of a carriage return (CR) or line feed (LF), or malformed encoded words {{RFC2047}}; added or changed addresses MUST be syntactically valid and subject to the MTA's relay and recipient policy; and body changes MUST produce well-formed MIME {{RFC2045}}.

## Unverified Data

Certificates that did not verify, proxy information, and client-supplied values such as the EHLO argument are assertions by the peer. Scanners MUST NOT treat them as authenticated identities. A trace identifier received from outside the MTA's administrative domain can be chosen by an attacker to link unrelated mail, so MTAs MUST NOT accept one from untrusted sources.

# Privacy Considerations

Hook requests can contain message content, addresses, client IP addresses, and authentication identities. MTAs SHOULD send only the properties a scanner needs, and the subscription mechanism makes this possible per stage and per part of the mail stream. Scanner operators SHOULD avoid logging content and personal data, and SHOULD limit retention to what their function requires.

# IANA Considerations

## Well-Known URI

IANA is requested to register the following well-known URI {{!RFC8615}}:

- URI suffix: mta-hooks
- Change controller: IETF
- Specification document: this document
- Status: permanent

## HTTP Field Names

IANA is requested to register the following field names in the "Hypertext Transfer Protocol (HTTP) Field Name Registry" ({{Section 16.3.1 of RFC9110}}), with status "permanent", no structured type, and this document as the reference: MTA-Hooks-Registration, MTA-Hooks-Request-Id, MTA-Hooks-Context.

## MTA Hooks Registries

IANA is requested to create a new registry group named "MTA Hooks" containing the four registries below. For all of them, new registrations require Specification Required {{!RFC8126}}, and the change controller of the initial entries is the IETF.

The designated experts SHOULD check that a name is a short identifier in the style of the existing entries and does not duplicate the semantics of an existing entry. For a stage, they SHOULD check that the specification defines when it is invoked, what the request contains, and which changes are permitted. For an action, they SHOULD check that its meaning is defined at every stage where it is permitted, and its place in the ordering of {{ordering}}.

### MTA Hooks Stages

Each entry gives the name, the direction (inbound or outbound), when the stage is invoked, the reference, and the change controller.

| Name | Direction | Invoked | Reference |
|---|---|---|---|
| connect | inbound | Client connected, before the greeting | RFC XXXX |
| ehlo | inbound | After each EHLO or HELO command | RFC XXXX |
| mail | inbound | After each MAIL command | RFC XXXX |
| rcpt | inbound | After each RCPT command | RFC XXXX |
| data | inbound | After the message content | RFC XXXX |
| route | outbound | Before each delivery attempt of a job | RFC XXXX |
| transmit | outbound | Before each SMTP transaction | RFC XXXX |
| delivery | outbound | After the attempts of a job | RFC XXXX |
| dsn | outbound | Before sending a generated DSN | RFC XXXX |

### MTA Hooks Actions

Each entry gives the name, whether the action is terminal, its rank in the ordering of {{ordering}} (higher is stricter), the reference, and the change controller. The meaning of each initial entry at each stage is given in {{tab-actions}}.

| Name | Terminal | Rank | Reference |
|---|---|---|---|
| accept | no | 0 | RFC XXXX |
| quarantine | no | 1 | RFC XXXX |
| reject | yes | 2 | RFC XXXX |
| discard | yes | 2 | RFC XXXX |
| disconnect | yes | 2 | RFC XXXX |

### MTA Hooks Session Counters {#iana-counters}

Each entry gives the name, its meaning, the reference, and the change controller. The reference of every initial entry is RFC XXXX.

| Name | Meaning |
|---|---|
| commands | SMTP commands received |
| errors | Commands answered with a 4xx or 5xx reply |
| invalidCommands | Commands that were unknown or syntactically invalid |
| rsets | RSET commands |
| transactions | MAIL commands accepted, including the current transaction |
| messagesAccepted | Messages accepted for delivery |
| messagesRejected | Messages rejected after DATA |
| pregreetBytes | Octets the client sent before the greeting |
| pipeliningViolations | Commands sent ahead of a reply without PIPELINING, or across a synchronization point |
| bareLineEnds | Lines ended by a bare LF or bare CR |
| firstCommandDelayMs | Milliseconds between the greeting and the first command |
{: #tab-counters title="Initial Session Counters"}

### MTA Hooks Error Codes

Each entry gives the code, the HTTP status it is used with, its meaning, the reference, and the change controller. The initial entries are those of {{tab-errors}}, each with the reference RFC XXXX.

